what do a t16 skyhopper toy imperial  inquisitors and the spice trade have in  common they're all easter eggs you  missed in obi-wan kenobi episodes one  and two warning spoilers ahead  obi-wan kenobi starts out in a flashback  set during revenge of the sith  specifically the brutal assaults on the  jedi temple led by anakin skywalker a  scene shows a class of jedi younglings  training with a master in the waves of  the force when clone troopers attacked  the class the master escorts her  students through the halls of the temple  taking down several attacking clone  troopers along the way tragically she  doesn't make it far before dying to a  well-placed blaster bolt leaving the  younglings to fend for themselves in  addition to showing the new angle on the  events of order 66 the flashback scene  includes a couple of details that most  fans of the star wars prequels should  recognize the helmets that the  younglings are wearing for instance are  the same ones worn by yoda's students in  attack of the clones the layouts of the  jedi temple as shown in the escape  sequence also perfectly mirrors his  portrayal and the prequel trilogy  finally the order 66 scene sets the  stage for the gross imbalance of power  rampant drone the imperial era some of  the younglings may have survived but  they would either become fugitives or  imperial inquisitors  what do we do now  we run  disney plus has returned to tatooine  again and again in his various star wars  shows and while obi-wan quickly makes  his way off the desert planet in his own  series he spends a whole first episode  wandering the dune sea at this point  jawas and panthers are a little too  commonplace to counter easter eggs but  there are still some fun details waiting  to be found the saloon that the  inquisitors infiltrate is a nice homage  to the mos eisley cantina complete with  bright green drinks served in tall  opaque glasses obi-wan's java friend  tikka references a gentleman wastes  which any good star wars fan knows are  not to be travelled lightly  additionally there's a good sampling of  the local tatooine wildlife  obi-wan earns a living by working on a  butcher line harvesting meat from the  massive corpse of what appears to be a  crate dragon or some similarly massive  beast his cruel overseer might conjure  memories of rey's portion paying bass in  the force awakens it's then revealed  that obi-wan's main mode of transport is  an eop a long-standed camel-like  creature first introduced in the phantom  menace eops have historically been best  known for their flatulence but obi-wan  seems to have required one with more  decorum  obi-wan kenobi episode 1 paints a pretty  grim picture of the jedi's life in exile  after a hard day at work he returns his  hideout in the desert which is little  more than a furnished cave still even  amid the barrenness obi-wan has retained  some reminders of his old life which  includes some interesting references to  the star wars movies at the entrance to  his home there's a periscope scanner  presumably a security protocol to alert  him to intruders fans may recognize the  device's design as an identical  periscope is used repeatedly by r2d2  throughout the star wars films hopefully  obi-wan's is simply a repurposed gadget  and not the appendage of some poor  astromech droid buried in the sun  later in episode 1 obi-wan takes out an  old hologram communicator to receive a  transmission from bail organa it's  another device that should be familiar  to those who have seen the movies as  obi-wan is seen using it in the prequel  trilogy perhaps most striking of all  however is the cloud of defeat that  follows obi-wan under his bend persona  the jedi truly looks like he's given up  he's buried his and anakin's lightsaber  in the desert in what is a possible  allusion to the end of the rise of  skywalker he's terrorized by nightmares  and he is hesitant to use the force in  basically any scenario given how calm  and controlled obi-wan is in a new hope  it's interesting to see him in such a  dark place  if you've absorbed much star wars media  in the disney era then you're probably  familiar with the imperial inquisitors  moses ingram's reaver who takes the  villainous lead in the disney plus  series is a new addition but some of the  emperor's agents have appeared in the  past sung kang's fifth brother for  instance has featured in several  previous projects including the darth  vader comics and star wars rebels he  eventually meets his end at the hands of  darth maul a curious twist to fate given  their shared history of hunting for  obi-wan the grand inquisitor another  prominent villain from the star wars  comics and star wars rebels also makes  his presence felt in the obi-wan show  however he's a bit different this time  around in star wars rebels the grand  inquisitor is voiced by jason isaacs and  some fans were upset when he was recast  with rupert friend for the live-action  version after being stabbed by reaver in  a surprising betrayal though the grand  inquisitor's fade seems up in the air  it's possible that he'll survive the  attack but it's also possible that  friend's character is a different  version of the villain and the isaac's  rendition in rebels might be some sort  of clone it is star wars after all  regardless both the fifth brother and  the grand inquisitor wield the same  spinning lightsabers first shown in star  wars rebels and they look a bit more  intimidating in live action  one of the more touching moments in  obi-wan kenobi episode 1 comes drawing  ben's interaction with tikka the jawa  he spends a good portion of his  hard-earned money to buy a toy for the  young luke skywalker and not just any  toy either specifically obi-wan  purchases a model of a t-16 skyhopper  a small airspeeder first mentioned in  the original star wars luke references  the craft during the briefing for the  first death star attack claiming that  but it's not impossible i used to  bullseye womp rats in my t-16 back home  he's also shown playing with a toy  version of the speeder during an early  conversation with c-3po it seems that  obi-wan was the giver of that gift all  along which is a fun little twist on a  tiny piece of pre-existing lore though  luke's uncle owen insists that obi-wan  takes the toy back it seems that he  acquiesces in the end  a lot of star wars media has dealt with  luke's time on tatooine but far less  time has been spent exploring his sister  leia's upbringing on old iran obi-wan  kenobi addresses that by spending time  on the planet following a ten-year-old  layer through the ins and outs of her  real life young star vivian lyra blair  does an excellent job bringing the young  princess to life  complete with leia's signature snark  stubbornness and wet in one scene while  climbing a tree with her little droid  friend lola leia starts naming the ships  she sees leaving the local spaceport  napoleon ranger probably scouting for  murson pirates  the mention of aquilian rangers is a  deep cut reference to the aquilae first  mentioned in an infamous cut line from  the original star wars the sun pirates  appear in some early star wars comics  that have since been relegated to the  legends timeline though they have also  been referenced in canonical media it's  also worth noting that in an attempt to  avoid her adoptive parents impending  political reception leia sends a young  decoy to be dressed and ornamented in  her place the exact same tactic her  mother padme used many times throughout  her political career  leia quickly reminds obi-wan of padme  after they meet and it's clear why  soon after arriving on the crime-ridden  world of daiyu obi-wan sees a face he  certainly didn't expect  that of an old clone trooper begging for  money on the side of a dirty street  though the former republic soldier is  technically much younger than obi-wan he  looks notably older as his body has  broken down due to the camino wins  accelerated aging alterations it's a  powerful reminder of what happened to  the clones after the rise of the empire  as shown in star wars the bad batch most  were quickly phased out of military  service it's clearly jarring for obi-wan  to see the old clone which makes sense  giving his complicated history with them  on the one hand he probably saw his old  friends cody and rex in the beggar's  face and he felt a deep sadness on the  other hand the events of order 66 would  surely have created a traumatic  connection to the clones obi-wan moves  on from the encounter quickly but it  surely stays with him especially since  the clone's blue accented armor is  remarkably similar to rex's  if there's underworld activity happening  in a star wars story there's a good  chance it has something to do with spice  the all-purpose drug signifier taken  straight from june that rule holds true  in obi-wan kenobi as well with the spice  trade rearing its head immediately on  dayu a young dealer tries to sell the  rogue jedi some of the stuff naming a  few different varieties including kessel  a primary space planet most famous for  the kessel run and felucian a reference  to the planet felucia where jedi ailer  sakura met her in fortune attend drawing  order 66. later on obi-wan infiltrates a  spice lab and even uses a vial of the  stuff to fend off a group of would-be  captors he smashes the small glass  container on the ground and locks his  attackers in with it sending them all on  what looks to be a pretty intense trip  less an easter egg than a fun little  detail this one's still worth mentioning  given the main character in the series  in the original star wars ben kenobi  describes lightsabers in an interesting  way an elegant weapon  for a more  civilized day  this line is referenced again in revenge  of the sith after obi-wan discards her  used blaster and declares it so  uncivilized  it's interesting then that he used his  blasters so prevalently in the first  couple of episodes of his own show as he  doesn't want to out himself as a jedi  obi-wan hesitates to use either the  force or his lightsaber he instead  relies on his martial arts prowess and  all weapons he can find to keep the bad  guys at bay more importantly obi-wan  doesn't complain once about the blasters  he's forced to use almost as if he's had  to let go of his old jedi pride it's a  small detail but it helps build the  sense that he's really given up all hope  if obi-wan kenobi can use a blaster  without a snide comment things truly  have taken a dark turn  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  obi-wan kenobi are coming soon subscribe  to our youtube channel and hit the bell  so you don't miss a single one  [Music]
 

